* The modular exponentiation algorithm reduces the time complexity of the operation from O(n) to O(log n), which is essential for performance.
* We initialize a result variable to 1 and treat the base g as a long to prevent overflow during the multiplication of intermediate results before the modulo operator is applied.
* The loop continues as long as the exponent n is greater than zero, checking if the current bit of the exponent is odd to multiply the current base into the result.
* After each step, the base is squared modulo p, and the exponent is divided by two, effectively traversing the binary representation of the exponent.

